## Test Smell Audit — Academic Catalog (testsmells.org)

**Target:** `DataAccess.IntegrationTests/UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs`
**Framework:** MSTest 4.1 · .NET 10 · Integration tests (`[TestCategory("Integration")]`)

---

### Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High     | 3           | 3              |
| Medium   | 1           | 1              |
| Low      | 0           | 0              |
| **Total**| **4**       | **4**          |

---

### Findings by Severity

#### 🔴 HIGH — Sleepy Test

**Affected:** `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` (line 87)

```csharp
Thread.Sleep(3000);  // 3-second hard sleep
```

**Why it's a smell even in integration tests:** Hard sleeps introduce non-determinism (3 s may not be enough under load) and unconditionally slow the suite. Integration-test calibration does *not* exempt Sleepy Test — a polling/retry or async wait mechanism is always preferred.

**Fix:**
```csharp
var timeout = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5);
var deadline = DateTime.UtcNow + timeout;
while (DateTime.UtcNow < deadline)
{
    if (_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id)) break;
    await Task.Delay(100);
}
Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id),
    "Notification was not sent within the expected time.");
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Flaky failures in CI; wasted wall-clock time on every run.

---

#### 🔴 HIGH — Conditional Test Logic

**Affected:** `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` (lines 101–108)

```csharp
if (loaded is PremiumUser premium)
{
    Assert.IsTrue(premium.DiscountRate > 0);
}
else
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}
```

**Why:** One branch may *never* execute — the test silently passes via the wrong path, hiding a gap. The test name implies a single expected outcome but the code allows two.

**Fix:** Write two explicit tests (one per type), or assert the expected type deterministically:
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_WhenStandard_ReturnsBasicUser()
{
    var user = new User("eve@example.com", "Eve");
    _repository.Insert(user);

    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");

    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.IsInstanceOfType<User>(loaded);
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded.Name);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** False confidence — the test always "passes" regardless of which type is returned.

---

#### 🔴 HIGH — Assertion-Free Test (Unknown Test)

**Affected:** `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` (lines 113–119)

```csharp
public void BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }
    // ← no assertion
}
```

**Why:** If `Insert` silently drops records, returns garbage IDs, or corrupts data, this test still reports green. The `_RunsWithoutErrors` naming pattern signals "implicit no-throw assertion" — but that's not verified by the framework.

**Fix:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_PersistsAllRecords()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));

    var all = _repository.ListAll();
    Assert.AreEqual(100, all.Count,
        "Expected all 100 bulk-inserted users to be persisted.");
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** False pass — coverage looks complete but nothing is actually verified.

---

#### 🟡 MEDIUM — Assertion Roulette

**Affected:** `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` (lines 35–38)

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name);
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email);
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0);
```

Four assertions, zero message parameters. When one fails in CI, the log reads `Assert.AreEqual failed. Expected:<Alice>. Actual:<null>` with no human context.

**Fix:** Add message parameters:
```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded, "User should be retrievable after insert");
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name, "Name should persist unchanged");
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email, "Email should persist unchanged");
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0, "Auto-generated Id should be positive");
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Slower failure diagnosis, especially in CI-only failures.

---

### Smell-Free Patterns ✅

The following tests are well-structured:

| Test | Notes |
|------|-------|
| `UpdateUser_ModifiesExistingRecord` | Clear arrange-act-assert; focused scope |
| `DeleteUser_RemovesFromDatabase` | Single concern; meaningful negative assertion |
| `ListUsers_ReturnsAllInserted` | Count `3` is self-documenting (3 items just inserted) — not a Magic Number |
| `InsertUser_ConcurrentInserts_MaintainsIntegrity` | Appropriate use of parallelism for an integration test; count is self-documenting |

The class's use of in-memory SQLite (`Data Source=:memory:`) is **not** flagged as Mystery Guest — it's a hermetic, deterministic test resource and aligns with integration-test calibration.

---

### Prioritized Remediation Plan

| Priority | Smell | Fix | Effort |
|----------|-------|-----|--------|
| 1 | Assertion-Free Test | Add `Assert.AreEqual(100, ...)` | ~2 min |
| 2 | Conditional Test Logic | Split into two deterministic tests | ~5 min |
| 3 | Sleepy Test | Replace `Thread.Sleep` with poll loop | ~10 min |
| 4 | Assertion Roulette | Add message strings to assertions | ~3 min |

Fixes 1–3 prevent **false passes** — tests that look green but prove nothing. Fix 4 is diagnostic quality improvement.
